---
import CategoryLayout from '@/layouts/CategoryLayout.astro';
import { TOWEL_SPECS } from '@/data/specs';
import { categoryTitle } from '@/lib/seo';

const title = categoryTitle('Towels', ['625 GSM', '100% Cotton']);
---

<CategoryLayout
  title={title}
  description="Hotel towels wholesale: bath, face and hand towels in 100% cotton, 490 to 625 GSM, plus 1000 GSM cotton bath mats. MOQ 50 pcs."
  h1="Hotel towels"
  breadcrumb="Hotel Towels"
  specs={TOWEL_SPECS}
  images={['towel']}
  specLead="GSM is calculated from weight and finished size. Both are listed so the number can be checked."
  supplierHref="/hotel-towel-supplier/"
  supplierLabel="Hotel towel supplier"
>
  <p>
    Towel specs live or die on GSM, and GSM is the one number buyers routinely get wrong. It is
    grams per square metre, not per towel. Two bath towels can both be listed as 800 g and still
    be different cloths if one is 70 × 140 and the other is 80 × 160.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our bath towel is 80 × 160 cm at 800 g, which works out to about 625 GSM. The face towel is
    35 × 75 cm at 150 g, about 571 GSM. The hand towel is 35 × 35 cm at 60 g, about 490 GSM.
    The cotton bath mat is the densest thing on the line at 1000 GSM in 50 × 80 cm.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">What the GSM actually changes</h2>
  <p>
    Below about 450 GSM there is less cotton per square metre. It dries faster and wears
    through sooner, which suits budget properties and high-turnover rooms where laundry
    turnaround matters more than hand feel. Pile construction still decides how it feels in the
    room.
  </p>
  <p>
    550 to 650 GSM is the working range for a hotel bath towel: heavy enough to feel like one,
    light enough that a commercial dryer can keep up with the throughput.
  </p>
  <p>
    Above 700 GSM the towel gets plush and also gets slow. A 1000 GSM bath mat is fine on a
    bathroom floor. A 1000 GSM bath towel holds a lot of water and spends longer in the dryer,
    and the gas bill climbs with it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Cotton, and what we do not claim</h2>
  <p>
    All four weights are 100% cotton. We do not put fibre-length claims on the spec sheet
    because we have not tested for them, and a supplier who claims long-staple without a test
    report is telling you a story.
  </p>
  <p>
    What you can judge is the pile and the weight. Terry loop construction is what makes a towel
    absorb. Heavier pile absorbs more and dries slower. Send us the towel you are replacing if
    you want a like-for-like quote; matching by GSM alone gets you close, not identical.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Sizes and quantities</h2>
  <p>
    The four standard sizes above cover most hotel programmes. Larger bath sheets, smaller
    gym-size towels and wrapped amenity sets are cut to order. Tell us the finished size, not
    the nominal one; shrinkage is part of the cutting plan.
  </p>
  <p>
    Towels are ordered in dozens in most markets and per piece in others. Quote whichever way
    your system runs. We will not put a per-towel retail figure anywhere on this page, because
    this is a wholesale line and a retail number invites the wrong kind of inquiry.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Hotel bath linen</h2>
  <p>
    In purchasing lists, hotel bath linen is the bath, hand and face towels plus the bath
    mat. Suppliers split the term differently. Some quote towels only and bill the mat on a
    separate line, some count a face cloth and some do not. Put all four on one inquiry so
    the quote covers the room rather than part of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Order the towels and the mat together where you can. White towelling from separate dye
    lots can read slightly different under bathroom lighting, and a mat a shade off the
    towels is the sort of detail a guest notices before the thread count.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Bath mats</h2>
  <p>
    The cotton bath mat is 50 × 80 cm at 400 g. At 1000 GSM it behaves more like a small rug than
    a towel: it stays where it is put and takes longer to dry. That is usually what a bathroom
    needs, but a property with limited drying capacity should know before ordering a hundred of
    them.
  </p>
</CategoryLayout>
